  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions persist through the end of the week \nas temperatures gradually increase.\n\nOne last breezy day today, albeit with temperatures several\ndegrees warmer than previous days (closer to average). The\ntrough axis associated with the broad upper-low moving off the\nnortheast CONUS will push through the region today. This will\nbring one final round 15 to 20 mph wind gusts early in the day.\nLess cloud coverage today as a much drier air mass builds into\nthe region as well. \n\nBroad upper-level ridging building over the central Plains will \ngradually track east throughout the week, with surface high pressure \nlingering over the Mid-Atlantic for several days. Winds remain \nlight for the foreseeable future as this ridging builds overhead\nbefore transitioning to a zonal flow pattern potentially by the\nupcoming weekend. Overall, this will lead to a week of mostly\nsunny skies and increasing temperatures to well above seasonable\nnormals, potentially nearing 70F as we approach mid-to-late \nweek. Overnight lows remain in the 40s mid to late week. This\nwill be an extremely dry air mass as well, which could have \nsome implications regarding fire weather (see fire weather \nsection below). \n\nLooking towards next weekend, it is still looking quite dry at\nthis point, but there are some hints at some activity in both\nbranches of the jet stream going into early next week. At this\njuncture, not seeing any significant precipitation potential.",
